From 533edbff60ebfa1c33837bed01732121f91488a7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Han <danielhanchen@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 00:43:33 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] llama : MoE cache: copy memory-mapped experts that the device
 lookup cannot map

The device lookup maps the host experts into the device address space with
ggml_backend_host_map (cudaHostRegister / hipHostRegister). A model loaded
with mmap keeps them in a read-only file mapping, which often cannot be
registered: on ROCm hipHostRegister becomes a KFD userptr that is always
faulted in for writing (the read-only flag is the coarse-grained flag on
HIP), and on CUDA a read-only mapping needs cudaHostRegisterReadOnly, which
not every device or driver supports. The cache then fell back to host
lookups for every layer, losing most of the gain of the cache under mmap.

When mapping a group fails, its memory-mapped expert tensors are copied once
into one anonymous host buffer owned by the model (what --load-mode none does
for every weight) and the mapping is retried, if they fit in the available
system memory (MemAvailable within the cgroup limit, or the available
physical memory on Windows) less max(2 GiB, 1/16). The other tensors stay
memory-mapped. LLAMA_MOE_CACHE_MATERIALIZE=0 keeps the host lookups.

test-moe-cache-materialize checks the copy and repointing on CPU.
---
 src/llama-ext.h                      |   5 +
 src/llama-model.cpp                  |   9 ++
 src/llama-model.h                    |   5 +
 src/llama-moe-cache.cpp              | 231 +++++++++++++++++++++++----
 tests/CMakeLists.txt                 |   2 +
 tests/test-moe-cache-materialize.cpp | 113 +++++++++++++
 6 files changed, 334 insertions(+), 31 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 tests/test-moe-cache-materialize.cpp

